the second wave of kovat 19 will start  somewhere not everywhere a local  outbreak that get out of hand and  spreads so new research by Sky News is a  concern we've looked at the trends in  infection rates in local authorities  across England and Wales and they show  that in Blackburn with Darwen the rate  in the first two weeks of July was 34  percent up on the previous fortnight in  pendel the virus was spreading even  faster up 54 percent but our analysis  shows how quickly lockdown works  although Leicester still has the largest  number of cases for the size of its  population rates fell by 20 percent  other researchers have looked back at  the impact of the national lockdown  scientists at Imperial College London  tested more than 120,000 random people  across England in May these weren't  people who are in hospital or in care  homes these were in the community so  that people most affected by the  restrictions on everyday life and the  researchers found the virus was in rapid  decline with cases harvick every eight  or nine days which puts the are number  at 0.57 well below the threshold of one  above which of course the epidemic  starts to grow  now that's lower than the estimate by  government scientists at the time they  put it at between 0.7 and 0.9 but that  included care homes where the virus  continued to spread at a high rate for  longer overall 13 in every 10,000 people  in England tested positive for the virus  in May rates were highest in young  people 27 cases in every 10,000 compared  to just seven in 10,000 in the over 65  they're more likely to have been  isolating people of Asian ethnicity were  also more likely to be infected with 24  cases in every 10,000 now strikingly 69%  of those testing positive didn't have  any symptoms in the week before their  test it underlines that many people  aren't even aware they're infected so  continue their normal daily activity and  they wouldn't be picked up by the test  and  system and that significant concern when  we're trying to keep a lid on such a  highly infectious virus
 

